function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') { // Set default order to ascending
    $size = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = $i + 1; $j < $size; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'ASC' && $set[$i]['age'] > $set[$j]['age']) || ($order == 'DESC' && $set[$i]['age'] < $set[$j]['age'])) {
                $temp = $set[$i];
                $set[$i] = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `$set`, which is an array of associative arrays, and `$order`, which is an optional parameter with a default value of 'ASC' (ascending). The function uses a nested loop to compare adjacent elements in the array based on their age values and switches them accordingly to sort the array.

 In JavaScript, a Promise is an object that represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. It's like a container for a value that may not be available yet, but will be resolved at some point in the future.

Imagine you order a pizza from your favorite pizzeria. When you place the order, you don't get the pizza right away, but instead, you receive a promise that the pizza will be ready soon. This promise can be in one of two states: "fulfilled" when the pizza is ready to be picked up, or "rejected" if something went wrong during the preparation process.

When a Promise is fulfilled, it returns a value (the pizza). If it's rejected, it throws an error. Promises allow you to write cleaner, more readable code by handling asynchronous operations using a chain of functions called "chaining."

Here's how you can create and use a simple Promise:

```javascript
const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // Asynchronous operation
  setTimeout(() => {
    const randomNum = Math.random();

    if (randomNum > 0.5) {
      resolve('Success!');
    } else {
      reject(new Error('Error occurred.'));
    }
  }, 1000);
});

myPromise
  .then((value) => {
    console.log(`Promise resolved with value: ${value}`);
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.log(`Promise rejected with error: ${error.message}`);
  });
```

In this example, we create a Promise that resolves after a delay with a random number. Depending on whether the number is greater than 0.5 or not, the Promise will either be resolved or rejected. We then use `.then()` to handle the resolved value and `.catch()` to handle any errors.

