 Here's an example of a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable and either calls the next middleware function or returns `false`, based on the value of the `NODE_ENV`:

```typescript
import { createParamDecorator, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

export const CheckNodeEnv = () => {
  return createParamDecorator(
    (data: unknown, ctx: ExecutionContext) => {
      const req = ctx.switchToHttp().getRequest();
      const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

      // If NODE_ENV is set to 'true', call the next middleware function
      if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
        return req.next();
      }

      // Otherwise, return false
      return false;
    },
  );
};
```

This decorator uses the `createParamDecorator` function from Nest.JS to create a new decorator called `CheckNodeEnv`. The decorator accesses the `NODE_ENV` variable using `process.env.NODE_ENV` and then determines whether to call the next middleware function or return `false` based on its value.

To use this decorator, you can apply it as a parameter decorator to any middleware function:

```typescript
@Controller()
export class AppController {
  @Get('/')
  @UseMiddleware(CheckNodeEnv())
  getHello(@Req() req: Request, @Res() res: Response) {
    res.send('Hello World!');
  }
}
```

In this example, the `getHello` method is decorated with both the `@Get` decorator and the `CheckNodeEnv` decorator. When a request comes in to the `/` endpoint, the middleware function decorated with `CheckNodeEnv` will be executed first. It will check the value of `NODE_ENV` and either call the next middleware function (if `NODE_ENV` is set to `true`) or return `false`. Since we're using Nest.JS, returning `false` from a middleware function will cause the current request-response cycle to end and no further processing will be done.

